    {
      "object_id": "mathia_interpretation_62363972b5ccb99d55ec449d11d1058490c92805e007ac18e74be7bf8609581c",
      "object_role": "interpretation",
      "proposed_content": "The proof first uses smooth separability to disentangle the gcd-dependent weight from u and v, with derivative control retained for later summation by parts. A Vaughan-type identity then decomposes the Möbius coefficient and produces S1, S2, and S3: S2 is treated trivially; S1 is regrouped into bilinear variables and sent to the Deshouillers–Iwaniec averaged Kloosterman estimate; S3 uses that averaged treatment in the longer-A regime but switches to Weil's individual bound plus summation by parts in the short-A regime. Thus the identity's inferred gain is scale-adapted bilinear structure, not mere rewriting, and no single cancellation estimate covers every range.",
