    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_c6dae7ed44cbddc3945741529dd006d1790a257be8a56c997df097f9f313e987",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The simple-zero argument adds a certification constraint to the total critical-line zero framework: after the general reduction counts zeros of Re eta, simple zeros are obtained only by subtracting zeros of eta and imposing a degree-one choice of Q so that surviving Re eta zeros imply xi=0 but xi' is nonzero. That restriction narrows the differential family used in the mean-value functional, forcing the simple-zero numerical optimization to be performed separately from the less constrained total-zero choice.",
